1
00:00:02,153 --> 00:00:05,279
Now with HTMX, if multiple requests are

2
00:00:05,327 --> 00:00:08,128
triggered around the same time and we only

3
00:00:08,161 --> 00:00:09,731
wanna send one of those requests,

4
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:13,335
we can achieve this behavior with help of

5
00:00:13,335 --> 00:00:15,381
another special attribute.

6
00:00:16,637 --> 00:00:18,318
And it's an attribute which I'll add to that

7
00:00:18,512 --> 00:00:19,271
form element here.

8
00:00:20,774 --> 00:00:22,665
And the attribute I'm talking about is the

9
00:00:22,859 --> 00:00:25,218
hx-sync attribute.

10
00:00:27,229 --> 00:00:29,842
This attribute exists to allow you to

11
00:00:30,146 --> 00:00:34,266
synchronize the sending or the handling of

12
00:00:34,378 --> 00:00:36,398
multiple requests at the same time.

13
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,518
Specifically, the idea behind this attribute

14
00:00:41,015 --> 00:00:43,211
is to make it easier for you

15
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:44,942
to cancel

16
00:00:45,503 --> 00:00:48,869
other requests if this element here sends a

17
00:00:48,869 --> 00:00:49,286
request.

18
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:54,352
Or to be precise, hx-sync actually takes a

19
00:00:54,352 --> 00:00:58,392
CSS selector that allows you to pick an

20
00:00:58,408 --> 00:01:01,072
element which might eventually send a

21
00:01:01,072 --> 00:01:01,794
request,

22
00:01:02,837 --> 00:01:06,624
and then you can tell HTMX what to do with

23
00:01:06,752 --> 00:01:09,705
that element's request if another request is

24
00:01:09,753 --> 00:01:11,646
sent from some other element at the same

25
00:01:11,646 --> 00:01:11,999
time.

26
00:01:13,042 --> 00:01:15,850
So here we could select our form by using

27
00:01:15,899 --> 00:01:16,813
this form selector,

28
00:01:18,065 --> 00:01:21,146
or since I'm actually already using hx-sync

29
00:01:21,274 --> 00:01:24,018
on the element which does send the request I

30
00:01:24,162 --> 00:01:26,510
wanna control, we can actually also use

31
00:01:26,655 --> 00:01:26,912
this.

32
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,359
That's something HTMX allows you to do.

33
00:01:31,358 --> 00:01:34,370
So with that, I'm telling HTMX that it's the

34
00:01:34,370 --> 00:01:37,324
request sent by this form element that I

35
00:01:37,324 --> 00:01:40,003
wanna synchronize with some other requests

36
00:01:40,003 --> 00:01:40,782
by other elements.

37
00:01:42,831 --> 00:01:45,143
And you then just have to tell HTMX what to

38
00:01:45,175 --> 00:01:45,288
do

39
00:01:45,903 --> 00:01:46,048
with

40
00:01:46,727 --> 00:01:50,236
this request here if some other requests are

41
00:01:50,850 --> 00:01:53,488
executed at the same time where this element

42
00:01:53,488 --> 00:01:54,626
here sends a request.

43
00:01:56,062 --> 00:01:58,219
You do that by adding a colon after your

44
00:01:58,267 --> 00:02:00,423
selector and then by specifying the

45
00:02:00,423 --> 00:02:01,164
strategy.

46
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:05,092
Now regarding the strategy, you got various

47
00:02:05,206 --> 00:02:06,928
options as you can see here.

48
00:02:08,366 --> 00:02:10,846
And in my case here, the strategy is

49
00:02:10,878 --> 00:02:11,442
replace,

50
00:02:12,151 --> 00:02:13,101
which simply means

51
00:02:13,842 --> 00:02:16,129
if this element here sends a request

52
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:19,130
and there are some other requests going on

53
00:02:19,341 --> 00:02:21,167
or started at the same time,

54
00:02:21,785 --> 00:02:25,133
those other requests will be replaced with

55
00:02:25,262 --> 00:02:26,637
this element's request.

56
00:02:27,785 --> 00:02:30,243
So they will be canceled and it's then only

57
00:02:30,275 --> 00:02:32,206
the request of this element that will

58
00:02:32,256 --> 00:02:32,783
survive.

59
00:02:34,807 --> 00:02:36,777
Alternatively, if I would specify abort

60
00:02:36,777 --> 00:02:37,656
here, for example,

61
00:02:38,225 --> 00:02:39,593
we would achieve the opposite.

62
00:02:40,869 --> 00:02:42,782
That would be another strategy which would

63
00:02:42,831 --> 00:02:45,230
make sure that the other requests continue

64
00:02:45,587 --> 00:02:47,694
but the request of this element would be

65
00:02:47,694 --> 00:02:48,116
aborted.

66
00:02:48,707 --> 00:02:50,868
Now that's not what I want here. Instead, I

67
00:02:50,966 --> 00:02:53,160
want the opposite. So replace is the

68
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:54,265
strategy I need here.

69
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:58,568
Well, and with this attribute added, we

70
00:02:58,584 --> 00:03:00,360
therefore now got a different behavior.

71
00:03:00,909 --> 00:03:03,686
If I reload and I again open that network

72
00:03:03,686 --> 00:03:05,930
tab so that we can see outgoing requests,

73
00:03:06,511 --> 00:03:08,691
of course, if I enter an email address here,

74
00:03:09,401 --> 00:03:10,612
that request is sent.

75
00:03:12,389 --> 00:03:13,745
But if I now enter a password,

76
00:03:14,309 --> 00:03:16,709
and I then don't tab out of that field or

77
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:19,092
anything like that, but instead I just hit

78
00:03:19,173 --> 00:03:20,373
Enter to submit the form,

79
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:23,185
you see this validate request here

80
00:03:24,683 --> 00:03:26,181
was actually canceled.

81
00:03:27,744 --> 00:03:30,210
You can also see the status here. It was

82
00:03:30,242 --> 00:03:30,933
canceled

83
00:03:31,688 --> 00:03:35,672
by HTMX and only that login POST request was

84
00:03:35,881 --> 00:03:36,138
sent.

85
00:03:37,215 --> 00:03:39,464
And that led to that validation and to that

86
00:03:39,464 --> 00:03:40,363
list of error messages.

87
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:43,205
But we now don't have that behavior we saw

88
00:03:43,205 --> 00:03:46,343
before where both requests would be sent and

89
00:03:46,424 --> 00:03:46,845
handled.

90
00:03:48,390 --> 00:03:50,344
And therefore, whenever you have a situation

91
00:03:50,344 --> 00:03:52,170
like this where multiple requests are

92
00:03:52,235 --> 00:03:54,125
started or sent at the same time

93
00:03:54,707 --> 00:03:56,419
and you don't want that to happen,

94
00:03:57,630 --> 00:04:00,064
you can use HX-SYNC to control which

95
00:04:00,178 --> 00:04:02,580
requests might be canceled and which

96
00:04:02,678 --> 00:04:04,382
requests will still be sent.
